Differentially Private Federated Learning with Flower and Opacus
Learn how to train federated models with DP-SGD using Flower and Opacus frameworks
Original photo by Mitchel Lensink on Unsplash . Edited by author.

In a previous post , I already described what Federated Learning was and gave an example of how to use it with the Flower framework. I also showed how to scale your experiments using multiprocessing to avoid cluttering the GPU memory. This tutorial is based on the previous one so I would suggest first review it quickly if you’re new to Flower and get more context.
A (really) short Introduction to Differential Privacy:

By shifting the flow of information exchanged between entities from data itself to only models parameters, Federated Learning (FL) achieved a higher degree of privacy than vanilla methods in Machine Learning. It proved highly useful for use cases that target mobile applications [ 1 ], as a large number of participants can potentially be available for training and offers possibilities to train on data otherwise unobtainable. However, FL by itself is not sufficient to achieve full privacy, as it was shown that models can memorize examples it was given instead of just learning how to use the features presented to achieve the task. This poses problems as membership inference attacks can be performed and recover original samples used to train the model.

A common approach to solve this issue is to encrypt the model’s weights before sending them to another entity (may it be a server or another peer). These techniques however can induce higher computational and communication costs to the workflow. An alternative is to use Differential Privacy (DP), which analyzes the potential privacy leakage induced by a given mechanism. DP was first introduced by Dwork [ 2 ] in 2006, it is based on the notion of adjacent databases, i.e. databases differing in at most a single element, and is defined as follows:
With D and D’ adjacent databases. Image taken from [ 3 ].

DP provides the guarantee that when adding or removing a given element in the database, the answer from our mechanism will not change by a definite amount: exp(ε) + δ. The common way to render a mechanism Differentially Private is done by adding independent Gaussian noise, with variance proportional to the sensitivity of the mechanism. To evaluate the privacy budget spent by a randomized mechanism, an analysis has to be performed so that we can keep track of the privacy loss. Such analysis includes the Rényi Differential Privacy (RDP) [ 3 ] and possesses a conversion to (ε, δ)-DP. One of the most advantageous properties of DP is that it is robust to post-processing, meaning that the privacy guarantee will stay the same whatever is done on the results obtained.

DP is a large subject and many others have written about it already so I will keep it short here, however, if you want a better idea of what it is in detail you can look at this other post on medium.

Now, one could decide to directly apply DP to the raw data to protect it. However, the addition of noise comes at a cost in accuracy, and the later DP is applied in our workflow, the better it is. This is why a common approach for ML models is to use the DP version of the SGD optimizer, introduced by Abaddi et al [ 4 ]. This algorithm applies the noise not on the data nor the model’s parameters but directly on the gradient at every step of gradient descent. It also uses clipping to limit the influence of one individual element over the training process. This is the algorithm we are going to be using today, implemented in PyTorch’s team Opacus library, and using RDP as the privacy analysis.
Setting up an environment

The code is available on GitHub and details how to install the libraries. I recommend using a virtual environment built with Poetry , also used by Flower’s team.
