All the libraries needed are here: Flower (flwr), Torch + Torchivision, Numpy, and Opacus. Some others are for typing concerns. You can notice we imported FedAvg from Flower, which is the strategy used by the library to define how weights are updated in the federated process. We need to create our strategy to adapt to the DP case. From Opacus only two things are imported: the PrivacyEngine and the sampler. The engine will let us attach it to any torch optimizer to perform the DP-SGD steps on it. As for the sampler, we will see about it in a while. The next step is defining our device for the model:

Previously, we also had used CIFAR10 as our dataset but kept it whole for simplicity. However, to appreciate more the addition of DP to our workflow we will create our own federated dataset. Centralized datasets benefit from being in most cases Identically and Independently Distributed (IID), meaning that each class is balanced and that samples within them share the same feature distribution. However, in federated contexts, as data comes from different entities, chances of finding heavenly distributed local datasets are near zero. Taking the example of MNIST, if we were to separate it based on each person that participated in its creation, we would notice that the number of digits drawn can vary between each individual but also that their writing styles are different. This is an example of a non-IID case but there are many ways a dataset can be non-IID and it is the main concern of federated applications as we are not able to see the data we are using. For our tutorial, we will keep it simple and will just separate CIFAR10 dataset among our N clients with a variation in the number of samples they can hold. Starting with the split function:

Each split initially contains the same number of samples, we then adjust it by alternatively removing samples from one split and adding them to another. We bound this with a ratio determining the minimum number of samples one split can have. A ratio of 75% means that our smallest possible split contains 25% of the average. Next, we load the data:

To load a client’s data we first load CIFAR10 and normalize it, we then create the splits based on the number of clients we want to use. Then a subset is created before feeding it to the DataLoader class. This is not the most optimal way, but since CIFAR10 is a pretty small dataset we can afford it. You notice here the use of the UniformWithReplacementSampler, it is also present in all Opacus’ tutorials but they never quite explain why they are using it. It is a really small step but an important one as this sampler allows us to modify the way batches will be created. In terms of training performances, we won’t even see the difference, without it however, the DP guarantee doesn’t hold. If nothing is done, batches will be selected sequentially, until there are no elements left in the dataloader. The problem is that DP bases its guarantee on the fact that, for every batch, each sample has the same probability p to be sampled. In one epoch, a sample can be seen multiple times and batches are not even sizes. The UniformWithReplacementSampler does exactly this, taking a sample with probability p (defined by the sample_rate argument) and putting it back in the dataset for the next batch. If not used, the resulting privacy budget given by Opacus will not change but will not be true.

Next is defining our model:

The model is based on the “ PyTorch: A 60 Minute Blitz ” tutorial. When using DP-SGD we have to take it into account when building models, as the noise and clipping induced by the algorithm can greatly influence the model’s behavior. For example, it is recommended to switch the ReLU activation functions, which are unbounded, by bounded functions such as Tanh [ 5 ]. This makes the model support better gradient clipping and improves performances. We don’t have any here, but dropping dropout is also a good choice, as DP is already a strong normalizer. If using any BatchNormalization layer in your network you should replace them with other ones (like GroupNormalization or LayerNormalization), because computing operations batch-wise is not DP-friendly and is not supported by Opacus. A final good choice, which has yet to be proven [ 5 ], can be trying to reduce the capacity of your model (its number of parameters). Overfitting with DP can be handicapping to your model so try to avoid it twice as much as usual. Likewise, hyperparameters should be fine-tuned for the DP setting.

