δ represents the probability the privacy guarantee given by ε doesn’t hold, so ideally it should be lower than the inverse of the dataset size. It is commonly set an order of magnitude lower.

Next is our training function:

We are going to review some important steps here. First, you can notice there are two batch sizes: vbatch_size (v for virtual) and batch_size . vbatch_size is our target batch size, while batch_size will be the actual batch size used by the dataloader. Using these allows decomposing the optimizer step with the PrivacyEngine in two parts: computing gradients and then applying noise and gradient descent. This gives us two advantages, first, the actual batch size given by the sampler we created earlier is not exactly the one we give. So accumulating gradients across multiple batches allows us to tend towards our initial target value. Secondly, using DP-SGD adds a computational overhead to the training process, requiring more memory as the batch size grows. As Opacus stated in their tutorials:

“Opacus computes and stores per sample gradients, so for every normal gradient, Opacus will store n=batch_size per-sample gradients on each step, thus increasing the memory footprint by at least O(batch_size) . In reality, however, the peak memory requirement is O(batch_size^2) compared to non-private model. This is because some intermediate steps in per sample gradient computation involve operations on two matrices, each with batch_size as one of the dimensions.”

This means that by keeping a small batch size for virtual steps, we can effectively manage the memory requirements for any batch size. In the code, it translates to determining the number of accumulative steps needed ( n_acc_steps ) and later when we compute the optimizer step we either perform a virtual step or a normal step and flush the gradients:

 if ((i + 1) % n_acc_steps == 0) or ((i + 1) == len(train_loader)): optimizer.step() # real step optimizer.zero_grad() else: optimizer.virtual_step() # take a virtual step 

Then you notice that we are loading the data and the model inside the training function. This is because we will use multiprocessing to allow GPU memory to be freed every time new clients are selected. If we were in a real setting, clients would load them before connecting to the server. You can also notice the optimizer was changed from SGD with momentum to Adam. Opacus works with any optimizer but I found personally Adam to be easier to play around with DP settings.

The alpha list contains the orders for the RDP analysis performed by Opacus. This list was taken from Opacus’ tutorials, in general, you don’t need to modify it but if you see that the selected alpha in your case keeps being one of the extreme values you might want to adjust it.

Next up is the PrivacyEngine, the custom object from Opacus that allows performing DP-SGD. The way you use it is by attaching it to any optimizer. It takes for parameters the model, the sample rate, the orders alphas, the noise multiplier (amount of noise added), the maximum gradient norm (clipping), and the target δ value. By giving them these parameters, it will perform RDP analysis and provide an ε value for the current state of the training process. This value is computed given the total number of steps performed on the dataset, these steps are stored inside the state_dict of the engine and can be obtained by calling the associated method. This is how we will keep track of the privacy budget of a client through the federated training. Every time a client is selected, they will store the resulting state dict in order to recover it the next time they participate.

Last thing I want to point out is that we’re only training for a single local epoch. The reason for that is because FedAvg was proven to not converge on non-IID datasets [ 6 ].

After training we define testing which doesn’t include any DP step:

Finally we need to define a behavior for our server to manage the clients. We could just use the standard Federated Average strategy but if we aim to respect a desired privacy budget we need to handle clients that go over it:

We only add a parameter called max_epsilon to keep track of the global privacy budget. Thanks to the composition theorem, the resulting privacy budget of the combination of independent mechanisms will be equal to the maximum value.

Each client will return in their metric dictionary whether their privacy budget goes over the target value. If so the server disconnects them so they don’t have to participate again in the training.

Lastly, configure_evaluate in the original FedAvg class is called at every round and if no evaluation function is passed to the strategy initially, it will select some clients to perform an evaluation. As well, at the last round, it will perform an evaluation on every client available. We want to avoid these two behaviors as we perform evaluation on the server-side, we however print a message if not enough clients are available for training since we disconnect some of them.
