With only two clients and a dataset that is only slightly non-IID, the model should perform close if not the same as if not using FL. After training for some time you may see that the network get stuck below 40% and struggles to get any higher. This is because the model we are using itself can’t perform that well without DP (around 55%). You can alleviate this by using a better model (augmenting number of filters per convolutional layers) or even using transfer learning. Feel free to experiment!
Conclusion and perspectives

With this post, you gained some insights on how to perform DP-SGD on federated models using Flower and Opacus. However, there are some parts we didn’t cover. For example, finding hyperparameters for federated DP-SGD is not tackled. One could choose to either compute them firsthand before training on each client, resulting in an additional privacy cost, or they could choose to fine-tine beforehand on a similar local dataset and use the hyperparameters found for all clients. We could also wonder about the impact DP-SGD truly has on our models and the real interpretation an (ε, δ)-DP budget truly means in terms of protection, [ 7 ] provides some answers to this but it is still an open topic. Finding architectures more resilient to the noise added and thus reducing the decrease in performance caused by applying DP is also important.

If you want to learn more about how to apply Opacus to different cases you can check out their tutorials section, you have there two additional examples for NLP. The same goes for Flower , although we used it here with PyTorch, it is not limited to it and you could experiment with any framework of your liking, even Scikit-learn . As well, we only covered a basic dataset for our case but one can use some specifically designed for the federated case like the LEAF benchmark.

This concludes this tutorial. Code is available here . I hope it will be useful and help some of you to understand better how Flower and Opacus can work together. Don’t hesitate to leave feedback and/or questions!
